WEBVTT

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:03.750
Rivers can grind walls of rock into dust.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.451
They carry millions of tonnes of soil

00:00:06.501 --> 00:00:08.941
and sand, and hold enough energy

00:00:08.991 --> 00:00:10.380
to power entire cities.

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:12.140
There are long rivers,

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:14.340
wide rivers and deep rivers.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:17.425
But by almost any measure, one river

00:00:17.475 --> 00:00:20.071
stands above them all: the Amazon.

00:00:20.821 --> 00:00:22.081
Its story begins high

00:00:22.131 --> 00:00:23.821
in the Andes Mountains,

00:00:23.871 --> 00:00:25.074
where water from glaciers

00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:27.831
and snowfields trickles down the slopes.

00:00:27.881 --> 00:00:28.878
Mountain streams such

00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:31.431
as the Urubamba River join together,

00:00:31.481 --> 00:00:33.556
forming the headwaters that will become

00:00:33.606 --> 00:00:35.302
the greatest river on Earth.

00:00:36.052 --> 00:00:38.182
As it crosses South America, the Amazon

00:00:38.232 --> 00:00:41.611
is fed by more than 1,100 tributaries,

00:00:41.661 --> 00:00:45.102
more than any other river in the world.

00:00:45.152 --> 00:00:47.922
Some arrive as thundering waterfalls,

00:00:47.972 --> 00:00:49.554
like those that carry water down

00:00:49.604 --> 00:00:51.373
from the Guiana Highlands.

00:00:52.123 --> 00:00:53.558
The Nile is usually

00:00:53.608 --> 00:00:55.393
considered slightly longer.

00:00:55.443 --> 00:00:56.764
But in volume,

00:00:56.814 --> 00:00:59.043
the Amazon leaves it far behind.

00:00:59.093 --> 00:01:00.000
Every second, the Nile pours about 5,100
